**1-Week Focused Plan** (Campus Placement Ready)

This is realistic for a final-year B.Tech student. ~4–6 focused hours per day. Priority order is based on how much it closes the gap with the Amgen JD.

---

### Day 1–2: Microsoft Copilot Studio + Power Automate (Highest Priority)

**Goal**: Be able to talk confidently about enterprise Copilot and show you understand the Microsoft ecosystem (this is the biggest JD mismatch right now).

**Best Resources** (all free):
- **Copilot Studio Agent Academy** (Microsoft’s own free curriculum) → https://aka.ms/agent-academy  
  Start with the **Recruit** track.
- Microsoft Learn path: “Create agents with Microsoft Copilot Studio – Online Workshop” → https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/paths/power-virtual-agents-workshop/
- Power Automate basics: Search “Use Copilot in Power Automate” on Microsoft Learn.

**What to actually do**:
1. Create a free Microsoft account / Power Platform developer environment if you don’t have one.
2. Build **one simple agent** in Copilot Studio (even a basic FAQ bot).
3. Create **one Power Automate flow** that does something useful (e.g., when a form is submitted → call an HTTP endpoint or send notification).
4. Note the difference between:
   - GitHub Copilot (what you already have)
   - Microsoft 365 Copilot
   - Copilot Studio (low-code agents)

**Output by end of Day 2**: Be able to explain in 2 minutes what Copilot Studio is and how it differs from just calling GPT via API.

---

### Day 3: Proper Git Workflow + Collaboration

**Goal**: Move from “I only do add + commit” to real branching + Pull Request workflow.

**Best Resources**:
- Official GitHub Quickstart for Pull Requests → https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/get-started/pull-request-quickstart
- Interactive visual practice: https://pcottle.github.io/learnGitBranching/ (do the main tutorial levels)
- First Contributions repo (optional but excellent practice) → https://github.com/firstcontributions/first-contributions

**What to actually do**:
1. On one of your existing projects (preferably the AI Document POC):
   - Create a new branch (`git checkout -b feature/improve-chunking` or similar)
   - Make a small meaningful change
   - Push the branch
   - Open a Pull Request on GitHub (even if it’s just you reviewing yourself)
2. Practice writing a proper commit message and PR description.
3. Learn these commands properly: `git switch`, `git branch`, `git merge`, `git pull --rebase`, resolving a simple conflict.

**Output**: One real PR link you can show if asked.

---

### Day 4: RAG Deepening – Chunking + Grounding

**Goal**: Strengthen the project you already have so you can speak about it at a deeper level.

You already have embeddings + pgvector + cosine search. Now add the missing conceptual + practical pieces.

**Best Resources**:
- “RAG Chunking Strategies: The 2026 Benchmark Guide” → https://www.premai.io/blog/rag-chunking-strategies-the-2026-benchmark-guide/
- LangChain RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter docs (still the practical default)
- Any short video/article on “grounding” (force the LLM to answer only from retrieved context + cite sources)

**What to actually do**:
1. Understand and be able to explain:
   - Fixed-size vs Recursive vs Semantic chunking
   - Why overlap matters
   - Typical good starting point in 2026: ~400–512 tokens + 10–20% overlap
2. In your existing AI Document POC, add or improve chunking (even if you just document what better chunking would look like).
3. Add simple grounding: In the system prompt, clearly instruct the model “Only answer using the provided context. If the answer is not in the context, say you don’t know.”

**Output**: Be able to explain your RAG pipeline + what you would improve about chunking.

---

### Day 5: Prompt Engineering + Basic Evaluation

**Goal**: Move from “I just call the LLM” to deliberately designing and testing prompts.

**Best Resources**:
- OpenAI Prompting Fundamentals → https://openai.com/academy/prompting/
- OpenAI official best practices (still excellent) → search “Best practices for prompt engineering with the OpenAI API”
- GPT-5 prompting guide (if available on developers.openai.com)

**What to actually do**:
1. Take the main prompt from your AI Document POC.
2. Rewrite it properly (clear instructions, role, output format, constraints).
3. Test edge cases:
   - Empty document
   - Contradictory information
   - Question completely outside the document
   - Very long / messy input
4. Add simple evaluation: Manually check 10–15 questions and note where it fails.

**Output**: One improved system prompt + notes on what broke and how you fixed it.

---

### Day 6: Docker Compose + Env Vars + Secrets + Pandas Basics

**Morning – Deployment hygiene** (2–3 hrs)
- Write a proper `docker-compose.yml` for your AI Document POC (FastAPI + PostgreSQL + optionally Redis/N8N).
- Move all secrets/API keys into `.env` and load them properly.
- Make sure you can explain: why we use environment variables, what `.env` is, why we never commit secrets.

**Afternoon – Pandas + SQL revision** (2–3 hrs)
- Pandas: Read a CSV, handle missing values, basic groupby/filter, convert between DataFrame and records.
- SQL: Practice joins, GROUP BY + HAVING, window functions (ROW_NUMBER, RANK), basic indexing concepts.
- Resource: Mode SQL tutorials or LeetCode SQL (easy/medium) + any short Pandas crash course.

---

### Day 7: Integration + Mock Interview Prep

1. Connect the dots:
   - Update your AI Document POC readme with better architecture explanation.
   - Mention the improvements you made this week (chunking awareness, better prompts, docker-compose, etc.).
2. Prepare 3 solid explanations:
   - Walk through your AI Document POC end-to-end (2–3 mins)
   - Difference between GitHub Copilot vs Microsoft Copilot Studio
   - How you would improve the RAG system further
3. Do one full mock:
   - Explain projects
   - Answer “Why Amgen / Why this role?”
   - Technical questions on FastAPI, PostgreSQL, N8N, RAG, Docker

